\title{Quotient group}
\taxon{Theorem}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{If #{K \trianglelefteq G}, the set #{(G : K)} of left cosets of #{K} in #{G} is a group under coset multiplication, i.e. #{gK \circ hK = ghK}. This group is called the \em{quotient group} (or \em{factor group}) of #{G} by #{K} and is denoted #{G/K}.}

\proof{
\p{We need to check that coset multiplication is well defined, i.e. if #{gK = \hat{g}K} and #{hK = \hat{h}K} then #{ghK = \hat{g}\hat{h}K}.}

\p{By \ref{GP19}, #{gK = \hat{g}K} tells us that #{\hat{g}^{-1}g \in K}, and #{hK = \hat{h}K \implies \hat{h}^{-1}h \in K}. Now #{\hat{g}^{-1}g \in K}, so #{h^{-1}\hat{g}^{-1}gh \in K} since #{K \trianglelefteq G}, so #{\hat{h}^{-1}h\,h^{-1}\hat{g}^{-1}gh \in K}, i.e. #{(\hat{g}\hat{h})^{-1}gh \in K}, so #{ghK = \hat{g}\hat{h}K} by \ref{GP19}.}

\p{So coset multiplication is well defined. The group axioms now follow easily:}

\ul{
\li{By construction coset multiplication is closed, as #{ghK \in (G : K)} for #{g, h \in G}.}
\li{The identity is given by #{eK = K}.}
\li{#{(gK)^{-1} = g^{-1}K}.}
\li{\p{Associativity holds since it does in #{G}; to check:}
##{(gK\,hK)\,lK = (gh)lK = g(hl)K = gK\,(hK\,lK).}}
}
}
